WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.237
Each man looked as if he’d been punched, but with Long John, the shock seemed to pass almost instantly.

2
00:00:09.238 --> 00:00:16.323
"Jim," he whispered, passing me a pistol, "stand by for trouble and stick close."

3
00:00:16.324 --> 00:00:24.205
"So you’ve changed sides again," I whispered back as I untied the rope around my waist. Long John didn’t answer.

4
00:00:24.206 --> 00:00:30.560
Jumping into the pit one by one, the pirates threw the boards aside and began digging with their fingers.

5
00:00:30.561 --> 00:00:34.448
Tom Morgan found one small gold coin.

6
00:00:34.449 --> 00:00:40.237
"One stinking coin!" he shouted, as he tossed it to the nearest man.

7
00:00:40.238 --> 00:00:49.497
"Is that all?" George Merry roared. "That’s our seven hundred thousand dollars’ worth of gold?"

8
00:00:49.498 --> 00:00:57.669
"Dig away, boys," Long John said coolly, moving so that we were atop the steeper, harder-to-climb side of the pit.

9
00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:02.802
"You’ll find some tasty nuts down there too, I bet."

10
00:01:02.803 --> 00:01:08.126
"Nuts!" George Merry screamed. He pointed accusingly at Long John.

11
00:01:08.127 --> 00:01:13.128
"He knew the treasure was gone the whole time!"

12
00:01:13.129 --> 00:01:21.647
The pirates scrambled out, and there we stood—Long John and I on one side of the pit, the five angry pirates on the other.

13
00:01:21.648 --> 00:01:27.910
"Mates," George Merry said, "that swab brought us to this miserable island!

14
00:01:27.911 --> 00:01:30.892
Let’s kill him and his young friend!"

15
00:01:30.893 --> 00:01:35.228
He raised his arm to signal the others to charge.

16
00:01:35.229 --> 00:01:46.099
But just then—crack! crack! crack!—three musket shots flashed out of some nearby trees, and George Merry tumbled headfirst into the pit.

17
00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:51.873
The man with the bandage spun like a top and fell dead. The other three turned and ran.

18
00:01:51.874 --> 00:01:57.469
Long John took out his pistol and fired two shots into George Merry as he struggled to get out of the pit.

19
00:01:57.470 --> 00:02:02.693
"George, I reckon I’ve settled with you."

20
00:02:02.694 --> 00:02:09.155
The doctor, Abe Gray, and Ben Gunn emerged from the trees with smoking muskets.

21
00:02:09.156 --> 00:02:14.022
"Hurry, lads," commanded Dr. Livesey. "We have to beat them to the boats!"

22
00:02:14.023 --> 00:02:20.886
We set off at a fast pace, plunging into the woods, as Long John hobbled on his crutch behind us.

23
00:02:20.887 --> 00:02:28.459
His strength as he tried to keep up was astounding; he never stopped, even though his chest must have felt ready to burst.

24
00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:34.218
"Doctor! Wait!" Long John called when we finally paused for a moment.

25
00:02:34.219 --> 00:02:38.626
From the plateau, we could see the three pirates running toward Mizzen Hill,

26
00:02:38.627 --> 00:02:44.676
and since we were closer to the boats than they were, we waited until Long John caught up.

27
00:02:44.677 --> 00:02:53.407
"Thank you, Doctor! You came in the nick of time," Long John said. "So it was you, Ben Gunn!""Yes,"

28
00:02:53.408 --> 00:02:59.310
replied the marooned man, embarrassed. "How are you, Mr. Silver?" "Ben,

29
00:02:59.311 --> 00:03:06.071
Ben," Silver murmured. "Good to see you, mate." "Gray,"

30
00:03:06.072 --> 00:03:11.660
the doctor said, "run back and bring us one of the pickaxes they left behind." As

31
00:03:11.661 --> 00:03:16.328
we proceeded to the boats, the doctor told the story of the missing treasure. "Ben

32
00:03:16.329 --> 00:03:20.148
Gunn’s our hero. He found the skeleton. He

33
00:03:20.149 --> 00:03:25.761
found the treasure, dug it up, and carried it on his back from that tall pine to his cave. It’s been

34
00:03:25.762 --> 00:03:29.377
there for over two months." Bashful, Ben

35
00:03:29.378 --> 00:03:33.355
Gunn kept his head down as we walked. "I got

36
00:03:33.356 --> 00:03:37.724
this secret out of Ben after the attack at the stockade. The next

37
00:03:37.725 --> 00:03:44.951
morning, when I saw the ship was gone, I gave the useless map and possession of the stockade to Silver. We moved

38
00:03:44.952 --> 00:03:49.808
to the safety of Ben Gunn’s cave to guard the money. "As for

39
00:03:49.809 --> 00:03:56.926
you, Jim," the doctor continued, "I knew you would suffer when the pirates discovered the treasure was gone. So I

40
00:03:56.927 --> 00:04:04.144
left the squire to guard the captain in the cave, while Gray, Ben Gunn, and I raced to the tall pine. But you

41
00:04:04.145 --> 00:04:06.944
and the others were too fast. So Ben

42
00:04:06.945 --> 00:04:14.736
Gunn ran ahead and played the part of Flint’s ghost, which delayed matters until Gray and I could get into position." "Lucky for

43
00:04:14.737 --> 00:04:22.488
me Hawkins was there," said Long John. "You would have let them cut me to bits." "Without a

44
00:04:22.489 --> 00:04:26.332
doubt," Dr. Livesey agreed cheerily. We reached

45
00:04:26.333 --> 00:04:31.837
the boats, and the doctor, with the pickax Gray had, demolished one of them. We boarded

46
00:04:31.838 --> 00:04:36.227
the other and set out for North Inlet to check on the ship. As we

47
00:04:36.228 --> 00:04:42.147
passed Rum Cove, we saw someone on shore outside Ben Gunn’s cave. "It’s the

48
00:04:42.148 --> 00:04:46.637
squire!" I waved and started cheering: "Hip! Hip!—" "Hooray!" we

49
00:04:46.638 --> 00:04:50.344
all shouted. We finally

50
00:04:50.345 --> 00:04:55.976
sailed just inside the mouth of North Inlet, and there was the Hispaniola . "The

51
00:04:55.977 --> 00:05:00.962
tide lifted her!" I cried. "She’s standing straight as ever." "There’s

52
00:05:00.963 --> 00:05:05.356
a problem with the mainsail," Gray said. "But she’ll get us home. After

53
00:05:05.357 --> 00:05:10.094
I drop you off at Rum Cove, I’ll go back to the ship." So

54
00:05:10.095 --> 00:05:17.684
we rowed back to the cave. The squire came out to greet us, saying, "I’m glad you’re safe, Jim." Long

55
00:05:17.685 --> 00:05:20.816
John saluted politely. Trelawney

56
00:05:20.817 --> 00:05:24.957
gave him an angry look. "You’re a villain and an impostor. I

57
00:05:24.958 --> 00:05:29.006
was told not to prosecute you, so I won’t. But

58
00:05:29.007 --> 00:05:32.971
those dead men will haunt you." We

59
00:05:32.972 --> 00:05:38.794
entered the large, airy cave, and before a big fire lay Captain Smollett. In

60
00:05:38.795 --> 00:05:45.511
a far corner, shining in the firelight, were great heaps of coins and gold bars. "I

61
00:05:45.512 --> 00:05:49.715
know of seventeen men who died for that," I thought. "And

62
00:05:49.716 --> 00:05:57.897
how many more? How much blood, sorrow, and cruelty was involved? No one alive can tell." "You’re

63
00:05:57.898 --> 00:06:03.056
a good boy, Jim," the captain said, "but I don’t think you and I will go to sea again. Is

64
00:06:03.057 --> 00:06:07.745
that you, John Silver? What brings you here, man?" "I’ve

65
00:06:07.746 --> 00:06:13.412
come back to do my duty, sir," replied Silver. "Ah!"

66
00:06:13.413 --> 00:06:17.080
was all the captain said. That

67
00:06:17.081 --> 00:06:23.205
night, we dined on Ben Gunn’s salted goat meat, with fruit and wine from the Hispaniola .

68
00:06:23.206 --> 00:06:28.667
"No one has ever been happier," I declared, "than we are right now."

69
00:06:28.668 --> 00:06:38.773
And there was Silver in the corner, eating heartily and joining quietly in our laughter—the same eager-to-please seaman I’d met in Bristol.

